.TH USBRADIOPLUS.CONF 5 "September 2026" "USBRadioPlus alpha" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
usbradioplus.conf \- USBRadioPlus channel, hardware, and audio-processing configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/asterisk/usbradioplus.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
This file defines USBRadioPlus radio channels and their hardware, Asterisk,
duplex, diagnostics, and audio-processing profiles.  Every noise-reduction and
dynamics stage is disabled unless explicitly enabled.
.PP
This is a clean-slate configuration interface. It does not accept
.BR chan_usbradio
or earlier USBRadioPlus option names. Start a new file from the shipped sample.
.PP
A bare section name creates a channel.  For example,
.B [usb]
creates
.BR RadioPlus/usb .
The channel selects profiles with
.BR asterisk_profile ,
.BR hardware_profile ,
.BR receive_profile ,
.BR transmit_profile ,
.BR ctcss_profile ,
.BR dcs_profile ,
.BR duplex_profile ,
.BR diagnostics_profile ,
.BR local_profile ,
.BR link_profile ,
and
.BR voice_telemetry_profile .
A profile named
.B site
is written as
.BR [hardware site] ,
.BR [local site] ,
and so forth.  Omitted references select a profile having the same name as the
channel.  Several channels may reference the same profile.
.PP
Flat sections such as
.BR [hardware] ,
.BR [receive] ,
.BR [transmit] ,
.BR [ctcss] ,
.BR [dcs] ,
.BR [local] ,
and
.B [voice_telemetry]
provide shared defaults.  Settings in a channel's scoped or referenced profile
override the corresponding flat-section settings.
.PP
For \fB[receive]\fR, \fB[transmit]\fR, \fB[ctcss]\fR, and \fB[dcs]\fR,
an omitted option resolves to the concrete value in the shipped configuration
sample.  An inactive CTCSS or DCS section does not select that protocol; each
direction selects its signaling method explicitly.
.PP
Audio graph settings and live-safe mixer or signaling controls take effect on a
processing reload. Device selection, buffering, interface layout, and channel
construction settings take effect when Asterisk loads the module.
.PP
Reload validation is strict. An unknown section, unknown option, repeated or
unknown graph stage, option in an inapplicable chain, non-finite number, or
out-of-range value rejects the new file and leaves the running configuration
unchanged.
.SH NAMED CHANNEL OPTIONS
.TP
.B channel_enabled = yes|no
Enable this named radio channel. Default is yes.
.TP
.B asterisk_profile, hardware_profile, receive_profile, transmit_profile, ctcss_profile, dcs_profile, duplex_profile, diagnostics_profile
Select the corresponding settings profiles.
.TP
.B local_profile, link_profile, voice_telemetry_profile
Select the three source-chain profiles.
.SH ASTERISK OPTIONS
The flat or scoped
.B [asterisk]
section controls Asterisk's receive jitter buffer.
.TP
.B asterisk_jitter_buffer_enabled = yes|no
Enable the receive jitter buffer.
.TP
.B asterisk_jitter_buffer_max_size_ms = milliseconds
Set the maximum jitter-buffer length.
.TP
.B asterisk_jitter_buffer_resync_threshold_ms = milliseconds
Set the timestamp discontinuity that causes resynchronization.
.TP
.B asterisk_jitter_buffer_implementation = fixed|adaptive
Select the fixed-size or adaptive jitter-buffer implementation.
.TP
.B asterisk_jitter_buffer_logging_enabled = yes|no
Enable jitter-buffer frame diagnostics.
.TP
.B asterisk_jitter_buffer_force_enabled = yes|no
Force jitter-buffer use.
.TP
.B asterisk_jitter_buffer_target_extra_ms = milliseconds
Add target buffering when using the adaptive implementation.
.TP
.B asterisk_jitter_buffer_video_sync_enabled = yes|no
Delay video to remain synchronized with buffered audio. USBRadioPlus does not
normally carry video, but Asterisk accepts this setting.
.SH HARDWARE OPTIONS
The flat or scoped
.B [hardware]
section configures the CM119 converter and playback-channel routing.
.TP
.B hardware_input_gain_db = -30..30
Set CM119 capture gain. Zero dB is the normalized mixer midpoint, 500 on the
0--999 scale. Values beyond the available mixer range are clamped.
.TP
.B hardware_output_a_gain_db, hardware_output_b_gain_db = -30..30
Set playback gain for CM119 output A or B. Zero dB is the normalized mixer
midpoint.
.TP
.B hardware_output_a_assignment, hardware_output_b_assignment = off|voice|ctcss|voice_ctcss|auxvoice
Route no signal, voice, transmit signaling, voice mixed with transmit
signaling, or auxiliary voice to the corresponding CM119 output. A selected
CTCSS or DCS transmit method requires at least one output assigned to
\fBctcss\fR or \fBvoice_ctcss\fR.
.TP
.B hardware_ptt_inverted = yes|no
Invert the physical PTT output.
.TP
.B hardware_emphasis_corner_hz = frequency
Set the de-emphasis and pre-emphasis corner frequency in hertz. The value must
be greater than zero and less than 300.
.SS Device and buffering
The app_rpt-to-hardware-callback program ring is the only audio queue that
bridges the app_rpt and 48 kHz native-rendering clock domains. It renders from
its first available sample; it has no source-audio startup fill or playback
reserve. Its 110 ms occupancy target is used only by clock recovery, not as a
fixed latency or keyed-transmit admission delay. These properties are separate
from device-queue and IAX jitter-buffer settings. The ring is lock-free: its
Asterisk producer and native hardware-callback consumer never wait for one
another. One persistent libsamplerate stream converts the active app_rpt rate
to the 48 kHz hardware rate and gently adjusts that conversion ratio for
independent clock drift, including at a nominal one-to-one ratio. It does not
periodically repeat or skip PCM samples. If the producer is temporarily short,
the callback conceals the shortfall smoothly rather than abruptly inserting
silence.
.PP
App_rpt program frames, including idle silence, enter the stream regardless of
PTT. A native-parrot implementation may be the sole external program-audio
producer only while it supplies native-rate samples through that same lock-free
program ring. Legacy app_rpt echo is distinct and is not an external producer
under this exception. PTT transitions preserve the program-ring state. This
PCM buffering does not request or release PTT; app_rpt and tuning retain
ownership of the desired PTT state. After a complete non-silent DAC block is
accepted, USBRadioPlus keeps physical PTT asserted until the device's measured
or estimated playback delay plus one 20 ms native block has elapsed. It sends
silence while that output drains and cancels the hold for a new key request.
This is a best-effort guard against cutting off queued audio, not a second
transmit-control source.
.PP
The native hardware callback directly performs local receive processing, final
voice/telemetry processing, CTCSS/DCS rendering, and 48 kHz DAC rendering from
preallocated state. There is no native worker thread or native input/output
worker queue. Prepared filtering, RNNoise, and sample-rate conversion run in
that callback when configured; graph construction and reconfiguration do not.
.PP
Sound-device output runs continuously at 48 kHz, carrying silence while idle.
PTT, carrier detection, and CTCSS decode do not stop device writes. Existing
receive qualification and transmit muting still determine which audio is
audible. The OSS output writer adds no extra silence block when its queue empties.
.TP
.B hardware_device_identifier, hardware_serial
Select the USB audio interface by device identifier or serial number.
.TP
.B hardware_interface_type
Select the supported interface wiring layout.
.TP
.B hardware_eeprom_enabled = yes|no
Enable interface EEPROM tuning storage.
.TP
.B hardware_audio_fragment_count = integer
Set the raw OSS \fBSNDCTL_DSP_SETFRAGMENT\fR request. Leave it at the adapter
default unless the OSS device requires a documented override. It does not
configure the app_rpt program ring, and the PortAudio adapter ignores it.
.TP
.B hardware_audio_queue_size = integer
Set the maximum queued OSS playback fragments. This bounds OSS device latency;
it does not alter program-ring latency. It can change the best-effort physical
PTT drain time after a non-silent block because USBRadioPlus measures queued
OSS playback before releasing PTT. The PortAudio adapter ignores it.
.SH RECEIVE OPTIONS
The flat or scoped \fB[receive]\fR section controls receiver behavior.
.TP
.B signaling_method = carrier|ctcss|dcs
Select exactly one receive qualification method: carrier, CTCSS, or DCS.
Carrier uses COS only; CTCSS
requires a non-\fBno\fR CTCSS source and a supported
\fB[ctcss]\fR receive-frequency list. DCS requires a valid
\fB[dcs]\fR receive code. A reload rejects a selected signaling method whose
required inputs are absent or invalid. Default is \fBcarrier\fR.
.TP
.B audio_source = no|speaker|flat
Select the receiver audio connection. Default is \fBflat\fR.
.TP
.B cos_assignment = no|usb|usbinvert|dsp|vox|pp|ppinvert
Select the carrier-operated-squelch source. Default is \fBdsp\fR.
.TP
.B cpu_saver_enabled = yes|no
Reduce optional local receive processing while the receiver is unqualified.
When disabled, enabled local RNNoise and dynamics run on every native interval.
This control does not stop sound-device reads or writes. Default is \fBno\fR.
.SS Receiver and squelch
.TP
.B vox_hang_ms = 0..32767
Set nonnegative VOX hang time in milliseconds. Default is 2000 ms.
.TP
.B vox_threshold = 0..32767
Set the nonnegative VOX detector threshold. Default is 0.
.TP
.B noise_squelch_hysteresis = 0..32767
Set nonnegative native noise-squelch hysteresis. Hysteresis is additional
discriminator-noise margin while the gate is open, not a delay or a SINAD
setting. Default is 3000.
DSP COS follows a sample-rate MICOR-style fast detector and long-tail storage
state: abrupt loss of a signal with at least 20 dB noise quieting closes
quickly, while weak signals and gradual fading retain about 150 ms of hold.
The software receive audio gate updates within each 48 kHz frame; Asterisk
carrier notifications remain frame-based. Hardware local repeat bypasses
the software audio gate. Noise calibration and meter units are unchanged.
.TP
.B noise_filter_type = 0 or 1
Select native noise-detector filter 0 or 1. Default is 0.
.TP
.B squelch_delay_ms = 0..511
Set receive audio squelch-tail delay in milliseconds. Default is 0.
.TP
.B on_delay_frames = 0..3000
Set carrier acceptance delay in 20 ms frames. Default is 0.
.TP
.B polarity_inverted = yes|no
Invert receive signaling polarity. Default is \fBno\fR.
.TP
.B squelch_level = 0..999
Set the native carrier-squelch threshold. Default is 500.
.TP
.B frequency_hz = 0..2147483647
Set the receiver frequency as an integral number of hertz for signaling, remote
operation, and reporting. Default is 0.
.TP
.B lsd_polarity_inverted = yes|no
Invert received low-speed-data polarity. Default is \fBno\fR.
.SH TRANSMIT OPTIONS
The flat or scoped \fB[transmit]\fR section controls transmit behavior.
.TP
.B signaling_method = carrier|ctcss|dcs
Select exactly one transmitted signaling method: carrier, CTCSS, or DCS.
Carrier sends no continuous
squelch code. CTCSS requires one supported \fB[ctcss]\fR
\fBtransmit_default_hz\fR value; DCS requires a valid \fB[dcs]\fR transmit
code. A reload rejects a selected signaling method whose required inputs are
absent or invalid, including a CTCSS or DCS method without a hardware output
assigned to carry transmit signaling. Default is \fBcarrier\fR.
.TP
.B cpu_saver_enabled = yes|no
Reduce optional transmit signaling DSP activity while the transmitter is idle.
This control does not stop sound-device writes. Default is \fBno\fR.
.TP
.B preemphasis_enabled = yes|no
Enable transmitter pre-emphasis for a direct modulator connection. Disable it
when the radio supplies its own pre-emphasis. Default is \fByes\fR.
.SS Transmit timing
.TP
.B settle_ms = 0..2147483647
Set transmitter settling time in milliseconds. Default is 500 ms.
.TP
.B rx_blanking_ms = 0..32767
Set transmit/receive blanking time in milliseconds. Default is 0.
.TP
.B off_delay_frames = 0..3000
Set post-transmit receive delay in 20 ms frames. Default is 0.
.TP
.B polarity_inverted = yes|no
Invert transmitter radio-signaling polarity. Default is \fBno\fR.
.TP
.B lsd_polarity_inverted = yes|no
Invert transmitter low-speed-data polarity. Default is \fBno\fR.
.TP
.B frequency_hz = 0..2147483647
Set the transmitter frequency as an integral number of hertz for signaling,
remote operation, and reporting. Default is 0.
.SH CTCSS OPTIONS
The \fB[ctcss]\fR section configures CTCSS only when the selected direction's
\fBsignaling_method\fR is \fBctcss\fR.
.TP
.B receive_frequencies = list
Set a comma-separated list of supported CTCSS frequencies in hertz. Receive
CTCSS requires a nonempty list. Default is 100.0 Hz.
.TP
.B transmit_frequencies = list
Set an optional comma-separated receive-to-transmit CTCSS frequency map. It is
used only when both directions select CTCSS. In that case it must contain a
supported transmit tone for every corresponding receive entry. Default is
100.0 Hz.
.TP
.B receive_source = no|usb|usbinvert|dsp|pp|ppinvert
Select the receive CTCSS indication source. Receive CTCSS selection requires a
source other than \fBno\fR. Default is \fBdsp\fR.
.TP
.B receive_decoder_gain_db
Set decoder-only gain from -60 through +60 dB. It does not alter voice audio.
Default is 0 dB.
.TP
.B receive_override_enabled = yes|no
Bypass received-tone qualification when receive signaling is CTCSS. It is
ignored when receive signaling is carrier or DCS. Default is \fBno\fR.
.TP
.B receive_relax = 0|1
Select strict (0) or relaxed (1) CTCSS decoder talk-off tolerance. Default is
1 (relaxed).
.TP
.B transmit_default_hz = frequency
Set the default transmitted CTCSS frequency. It must name one supported CTCSS
tone. Default is 100.0 Hz.
.TP
.B transmit_peak_dbfs = -90..0
Set the direct peak level of the generated CTCSS PCM waveform. Hardware output
gain is applied later at the DAC and does not change this setting. Default is
-24 dBFS.
.TP
.B turnoff_mode = no|ctcss_phase_shift|ctcss_tone_remove|ctcss_tail_tone
Select no special turn-off, a phase-shift tail, early CTCSS removal, or a
replacement tail tone. A phase-shift tail applies \fBphase_shift_degrees\fR
when the tail begins. Tone removal suppresses CTCSS for
\fBtail_duration_ms\fR before PTT release. A replacement tail tone substitutes
\fBtail_frequency_hz\fR for CTCSS during that interval. Default is
\fBctcss_phase_shift\fR.
.PP
The configured CTCSS tail ends before logical PTT release. Physical PTT can
remain asserted briefly afterward while accepted DAC PCM drains.
.TP
.B phase_shift_degrees = positive degrees
Set the phase angle applied when a \fBctcss_phase_shift\fR tail begins.
Default is 120 degrees.
.TP
.B tail_duration_ms = 40..32767 milliseconds
Set the pre-unkey duration for every non-\fBno\fR tail mode. Default is 180 ms.
Zero is not a disable value; select \fBturnoff_mode = no\fR to disable CTCSS
tail signaling.
.TP
.B tail_frequency_hz = positive frequency
Set the frequency that replaces CTCSS during a \fBctcss_tail_tone\fR interval.
Default is 55 Hz.
.SH DCS OPTIONS
The \fB[dcs]\fR section configures DCS only when the selected direction's
\fBsignaling_method\fR is \fBdcs\fR.
.TP
.B receive_code = 000N..777I
Set the receive DCS code: three octal digits from 000 through 777, followed by
N for normal or I for inverted polarity. USBRadioPlus accepts the complete
syntactically valid range to support standard and vendor-extension DCS/DPL code
plans. Default is 023N.
.TP
.B transmit_code = 000N..777I
Set the transmit DCS code using the same syntax. Default is 023N.
.TP
.B turnoff_code_enabled = yes|no
Enable the 134.4 Hz DCS end-of-transmission tail tone. It is sent before PTT
release. A qualified DCS receiver clears after at least 100 ms of a coherent
134.4 Hz tail; short tones, normal DCS words, and broadband noise do not clear
the receiver. Default is \fByes\fR.
.PP
The DCS tail ends before logical PTT release. Physical PTT can remain asserted
briefly afterward while accepted DAC PCM drains.
.TP
.B turnoff_duration_ms = 150..200
Set the DCS end-of-transmission tail duration in milliseconds. Default is 180.
.TP
.B peak_dbfs
Set the direct DCS PCM peak from -90 through 0 dBFS. It is independent of
\fB[ctcss]\fR \fBtransmit_peak_dbfs\fR; hardware output gain is applied later at
the DAC. Default is -24 dBFS.
.PP
USBRadioPlus renders DCS as a native shaped NRZ/Golay waveform. Its fixed
spectral-shaping filter bypasses transmitter pre-emphasis and all speech
processing controls.
.SS Radio identity and auxiliary controls
.TP
.B hardware_repeater_number, hardware_area, hardware_user_key
Set the native signaling-system identity.
.TP
.B hardware_idle_interval, hardware_turnoff_count, hardware_voter_reporting
Set signaling cadence, turn-off behavior, and voter reporting.
.TP
.B hardware_clip_led_gpio = 0..8
Select the optional clipping-indicator GPIO.
.SS GPIO and parallel port
.TP
.B hardware_gpio_1_mode, hardware_gpio_2_mode, hardware_gpio_3_mode, hardware_gpio_4_mode, hardware_gpio_5_mode, hardware_gpio_6_mode, hardware_gpio_7_mode, hardware_gpio_8_mode = in|out0|out1
Configure a CM119 GPIO as an input or as an output with its initial state.
.TP
.B hardware_parallel_port_device
Set the parallel-port device path.
.TP
.B hardware_parallel_port_base_address
Set the parallel-port I/O base address in decimal or 0x-prefixed notation.
.TP
.B hardware_parallel_pin_2_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_3_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_4_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_5_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_6_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_7_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_8_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_9_assignment = out0|out1|ptt
Configure a parallel-port output and its initial state or assign it to PTT.
.TP
.B hardware_parallel_pin_10_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_12_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_13_assignment, hardware_parallel_pin_15_assignment = in|cor|ctcss
Configure a parallel-port input as a general-purpose input or assign it to COS
or CTCSS indication.
.SH DUPLEX OPTIONS
The [duplex] section controls radio-interface duplex behavior. Missing options
use their documented defaults.
.TP
.B duplex_radio_mode = 0|1
Select half- or full-duplex radio operation.
.TP
.B duplex_local_repeat_level = 0..999
Set local receiver-to-transmitter audio level.
.TP
.B duplex_local_repeat_mode = hardware|software
Select the CM119 mixer or native 48 kHz local repeat path.
.SH DIAGNOSTIC OPTIONS
.SS Transmit timing trace
For a short unkey test, enable Asterisk module debug level 5:
.PP
.nf
core set debug 5 chan_usbradioplus.so
.fi
.PP
Capture a logger destination that includes debug messages. Use millisecond
timestamps (logger.conf dateformat %F %T.%3q). URP_TXTRACE records identify
the channel, key/unkey requests, each native frame's CTCSS state and phase
change, audio writes, and the beginning and end of the HID PTT write.
OSS also reports queued bytes and estimated delay at 48 kHz, stereo,
signed 16-bit. PortAudio reports submission results, not playback position.
.PP
For post-audio PTT holding, OSS uses its post-write queue depth; PortAudio uses
the stream output-latency estimate obtained when the stream opens. Neither
measurement confirms actual RF or DAC playback time.
.PP
Transmit a brief carrier with CTCSS, then release. A render record with
phase_shift_degrees greater than zero marks the phase-shifted block. Add its queue delay to estimate
playback time and compare with hid_begin/hid_end ptt=0. This excludes
unreported device buffering. Write drops or failures invalidate this estimate.
HID completion does not confirm electrical PTT or RF state.
.PP
Disable tracing after the test, or restore the previous debug level:
.PP
.nf
core set debug 0 chan_usbradioplus.so
.fi
.PP
Tracing is off at normal debug levels. It changes no audio or PTT policy and
records no audio samples. Per-frame logging adds load; use short captures.
.TP
.B diagnostics_trace_type, diagnostics_trace_level, diagnostics_fever
Set native trace selection, verbosity, and low-level tuning diagnostics.
.SH GENERAL CHANNEL OPTION
.TP
.B channel_enabled = yes|no
Enable the configured radio channel.
.SH CHAIN AND GRAPH OPTIONS
.TP
.B enabled = yes|no
Enable the named chain.
.TP
.B stage_order = list
Set the comma-separated processing order. Permitted stages are equalizer,
expander, agc, deesser, compressor, and limiter. Each may occur at most once.
All defaults place deesser immediately before compressor. An enabled stage runs
only when listed.
.TP
.B input_gain_db = -30..30
Apply source input gain. In the local receiver, this gain runs immediately
after de-emphasis and before fixed filtering or dynamics. Link defaults to 0 dB;
voice/telemetry defaults to 6 dB.
.TP
.B output_gain_db = -30..30
Apply gain at the end of the chain. Local and link default to -6.2 dB;
voice/telemetry defaults to 0 dB.
.SH PARAMETRIC EQUALIZER
The three-band equalizer is available in every source chain and is reorderable
with AGC, expander, compressor, and limiter. It is enabled by default.
.TP
.B equalizer_enabled = yes|no
Enable the three-band equalizer. Default is yes.
.TP
.B equalizer_low_gain_db = -12..12
Set low-shelf gain. Default is 2 dB.
.TP
.B equalizer_low_frequency_hz = 20..1000
Set low-shelf frequency. Default is 500 Hz.
.TP
.B equalizer_low_slope = 0.1..1
Set low-shelf slope. Default is 0.7.
.TP
.B equalizer_mid_gain_db = -12..12
Set mid-band gain. Default is -0.5 dB.
.TP
.B equalizer_mid_frequency_hz = 100..4000
Set mid-band center frequency. Default is 1000 Hz.
.TP
.B equalizer_mid_width_octaves = 0.1..4
Set mid-band width. Default is one octave.
.TP
.B equalizer_high_gain_db = -12..12
Set high-shelf gain. Default is -1 dB.
.TP
.B equalizer_high_frequency_hz = 1000..5000
Set high-shelf frequency. Default is 2000 Hz.
.TP
.B equalizer_high_slope = 0.1..1
Set high-shelf slope. Default is 0.7.
.SH SPLIT-BAND DE-ESSER
The de-esser is available in every source chain. It detects and
attenuates a bell-shaped sibilance band without reducing the rest of the signal.
It is reorderable and defaults immediately before the compressor.
.TP
.B deesser_enabled = yes|no
Enable sibilance reduction. Default is no.
.TP
.B deesser_frequency_hz = 2000..8000
Set the detection and attenuation center frequency. Default is 4000 Hz.
.TP
.B deesser_width_octaves = 0.1..4
Set the detection and attenuation bandwidth. Default is one octave.
.TP
.B deesser_threshold_dbfs = -60..-1
Set the sibilance-band level that starts attenuation. Default is -18 dBFS.
.TP
.B deesser_ratio = 1..20
Set how strongly levels above threshold increase attenuation. Default is 3:1.
.TP
.B deesser_max_reduction_db = 0.1..20
Limit attenuation in the selected band. Default is 4 dB.
.TP
.B deesser_attack_ms = 0.1..100
Set how quickly attenuation begins. Default is 2 ms.
.TP
.B deesser_release_ms = 1..2000
Set how quickly attenuation ends. Default is 60 ms.
.SH LOCAL-RECEIVER NOISE AND SIGNALING FILTERS
.TP
.B rnnoise_enabled = yes|no
Enable RNNoise after receiver de-emphasis and squelch gating and before
dynamics. This applies only to [local]. Default is no.
.TP
.B receive_bandpass_enabled = yes|no
Enable the local-receiver brick-wall band-pass immediately after the squelch
gate and before PL filtering. Default is yes.
.TP
.B receive_bandpass_highpass_hz = 20..2000
Set the receive band-pass low-frequency cutoff. Default is 20 Hz.
.TP
.B receive_bandpass_lowpass_hz = receive_bandpass_highpass_hz..6000
Set the receive band-pass high-frequency cutoff. Default is 5000 Hz.
.TP
.B ctcss_filter_mode = disabled|notch|highpass
Choose the single PL-removal filter after the receive band-pass and before
RNNoise and dynamics. Notch follows and removes the currently
decoded PL tone. Highpass removes the subaudible band. Notch never runs in
series with a high-pass. Default is highpass.
.TP
.B ctcss_notch_width_hz = 0.2..10
Set each CTCSS notch's -3 dB width. Default is 5 Hz, providing at least 50 dB
rejection at the +/-0.5 percent CTCSS encoder tolerance limits.
.TP
.B ctcss_highpass_hz = 50..500
Set the cutoff for ctcss_filter_mode=highpass. Default is 300 Hz.
.SH AUTOMATIC GAIN CONTROL
AGC levels active speech using detector-band RMS and a smooth gain change.
Its two-pole FFmpeg detector filters do not filter the program audio. The
stage adds no lookahead or audio buffering, peak limiter, clipping, or
hardware gain adjustment. Existing compressor and limiter stages remain
separate. Inactive audio retains the last gain; it is not muted.
.TP
.B agc_enabled = yes|no
Enable automatic gain control. Default is no.
.TP
.B agc_target_dbfs = -40..-3
Set the filtered detector-band RMS target after applied gain, not a
full-band RMS or peak target. Default is -24 dBFS. The target must be above
agc_activity_threshold_dbfs.
.TP
.B agc_max_gain_db = 0..30
Limit gain applied to active weak audio. Default is 6 dB.
.TP
.B agc_max_attenuation_db = 0..60
Limit attenuation applied to active strong audio. Default is 6 dB.
.TP
.B agc_rms_averaging_ms = 10..5000
Set RMS detector averaging time. Default is 200 ms. Longer averaging reduces
gain movement between syllables; it does not delay program audio.
.TP
.B agc_gain_increase_db_per_second = 0.1..100
Limit how quickly gain rises for active weak audio after the hold interval.
Default is 2 dB per second.
.TP
.B agc_gain_decrease_db_per_second = 0.1..100
Limit how quickly gain falls for active loud audio. Reduction begins without
the gain-increase hold. Default is 6 dB per second.
.TP
.B agc_activity_threshold_dbfs = -100..-3
Set the input activity threshold, strictly below agc_target_dbfs. Default is
-50 dBFS. A separate 10 ms detector opens at or above this threshold and
closes below the threshold minus agc_activity_hysteresis_db. Inactivity
freezes gain without muting audio or resetting gain to unity.
.TP
.B agc_activity_hysteresis_db = 0..12
Set the activity detector's closing margin. Default is 3 dB.
.TP
.B agc_hold_ms = 0..10000
Require continuously active audio below target minus deadband for this long
before increasing gain. Inactivity, gain reduction, or return to the deadband
restarts the wait. This never delays gain reduction. Default is 500 ms.
.TP
.B agc_deadband_db = 0..6
Hold gain when the detector-band output is within this distance either side
of target. Default is 1 dB.
.TP
.B agc_sidechain_highpass_hz = 0|50..2000
Set the two-pole detector high-pass cutoff, or 0 to disable it.
Default is 800 Hz.
.TP
.B agc_sidechain_lowpass_hz = 0..3500
Set the two-pole detector low-pass cutoff, or 0 to disable it.
Any positive frequency through 3500 Hz is accepted. When both detector
filters are enabled, low-pass must be above high-pass. Default is 1500 Hz.
.SH DOWNWARD EXPANDER
.TP
.B expander_enabled = yes|no
Enable downward expansion to reduce background noise between words. Default is no.
.TP
.B expander_threshold_dbfs = -100..-10
Set the level below which expansion begins. Default is -55 dBFS.
.TP
.B expander_ratio = 1..10
Set reduction below threshold. Default is 1.5:1.
.TP
.B expander_max_attenuation_db = 0..40
Limit expander reduction. Default is 9 dB.
.TP
.B expander_attack_ms = 1..1000
Set how quickly the expander opens for speech. Default is 10 ms.
.TP
.B expander_release_ms = 1..10000
Set how quickly it returns to attenuation. Default is 250 ms.
.TP
.B expander_sidechain_highpass_hz = 50..2000
Set the expander detector high-pass cutoff. Default is 800 Hz.
.TP
.B expander_sidechain_lowpass_hz = expander_sidechain_highpass_hz..3500
Set the expander detector low-pass cutoff. Default is 1500 Hz.
.SH COMPRESSOR
Select single-band or three-band compression independently in each source
chain. Three-band mode divides the signal below 500 Hz, from 500 to 2000 Hz,
and above 2000 Hz by default. The upper band ends at half the source sample
rate, not at a fixed upper audio frequency. Each band uses its own RMS
detector; the single-band detector filters do not apply in three-band mode.
The FFmpeg crossover changes phase but adds no lookahead. Changing modes
retains the other mode's saved controls without applying them.
.TP
.B compressor_enabled = yes|no
Enable compression of strong speech. Default is no.
.TP
.B compressor_bands = 1|3
Select one full-band compressor or three independent band compressors.
Default is 3.
.TP
.B compressor_low_crossover_hz = 100..2000
Divide the low and mid bands in three-band mode. Default is 500 Hz.
.TP
.B compressor_high_crossover_hz = compressor_low_crossover_hz..5000
Divide the mid and high bands in three-band mode. Default is 2000 Hz.
This must be strictly above the low crossover and below half the active source
sample rate. An invalid active crossover prevents graph activation.
.SS Single-band controls
The following controls apply only when compressor_bands is 1.
.TP
.B compressor_threshold_dbfs = -60..0
Set the level above which compression begins. Default is -6 dBFS.
.TP
.B compressor_ratio = 1..20
Set the compression ratio. Default is 2:1.
.TP
.B compressor_makeup_gain_db = -30..30
Apply gain after compression. Default is 0 dB.
.TP
.B compressor_attack_ms = 1..1000
Set compressor attack time. Default is 75 ms.
.TP
.B compressor_release_ms = 1..9000
Set compressor release time. Default is 300 ms.
.TP
.B compressor_sidechain_highpass_hz = 50..2000
Set the compressor detector high-pass cutoff. Default is 800 Hz.
.TP
.B compressor_sidechain_lowpass_hz = compressor_sidechain_highpass_hz..3500
Set the compressor detector low-pass cutoff. Default is 1500 Hz.
.SS Three-band controls
The following controls apply only when compressor_bands is 3.
.TP
.B compressor_low_threshold_dbfs = -60..0
Set low-band compression onset. Default is -6 dBFS.
.TP
.B compressor_low_ratio = 1..20
Set low-band compression ratio. Default is 2:1.
.TP
.B compressor_low_makeup_gain_db = -30..30
Set low-band gain after compression. Default is 0 dB.
.TP
.B compressor_low_knee_db = 0..18
Set low-band soft-knee width. Default is 9 dB.
.TP
.B compressor_low_attack_ms = 1..1000
Set low-band attack time. Default is 75 ms.
.TP
.B compressor_low_release_ms = 1..9000
Set low-band release time. Default is 300 ms.
.TP
.B compressor_mid_threshold_dbfs = -60..0
Set mid-band compression onset. Default is -6 dBFS.
.TP
.B compressor_mid_ratio = 1..20
Set mid-band compression ratio. Default is 2:1.
.TP
.B compressor_mid_makeup_gain_db = -30..30
Set mid-band gain after compression. Default is 0 dB.
.TP
.B compressor_mid_knee_db = 0..18
Set mid-band soft-knee width. Default is 9 dB.
.TP
.B compressor_mid_attack_ms = 1..1000
Set mid-band attack time. Default is 75 ms.
.TP
.B compressor_mid_release_ms = 1..9000
Set mid-band release time. Default is 300 ms.
.TP
.B compressor_high_threshold_dbfs = -60..0
Set high-band compression onset. Default is -6 dBFS.
.TP
.B compressor_high_ratio = 1..20
Set high-band compression ratio. Default is 2:1.
.TP
.B compressor_high_makeup_gain_db = -30..30
Set high-band gain after compression. Default is 0 dB.
.TP
.B compressor_high_knee_db = 0..18
Set high-band soft-knee width. Default is 9 dB.
.TP
.B compressor_high_attack_ms = 1..1000
Set high-band attack time. Default is 75 ms.
.TP
.B compressor_high_release_ms = 1..9000
Set high-band release time. Default is 300 ms.
.SH LIMITER
This reorderable stage supports one full-band limiter or three independently
controlled bands. The fixed final transmitter limiter is a separate stage.
This stage uses peak detection and finite-ratio limiting, not an absolute
output ceiling. The fixed final transmitter limiter controls the transmitter
ceiling when enabled.
Three-band mode uses the same default divisions as the compressor: below
500 Hz, 500 to 2000 Hz, and above 2000 Hz up to half the source sample rate.
Its crossover settings are independent of the compressor's. Changing modes
retains inactive controls. The FFmpeg crossover changes phase but adds no
lookahead.
.TP
.B limiter_enabled = yes|no
Enable the shared FFmpeg limiter in the reorderable graph. Default is no.
.TP
.B limiter_bands = 1|3
Select one full-band limiter or three independent band limiters. Default is 3.
.SS Single-band controls
The following controls apply only when limiter_bands is 1.
.TP
.B limiter_threshold_dbfs = -40..-1
Set the limiting threshold. Default is -1.5 dBFS.
.TP
.B limiter_ratio = 1..20
Set the limiting ratio. Default is 20:1.
.TP
.B limiter_knee_db = 0..18
Set the soft-knee width. Default is 0 dB (hard knee).
.TP
.B limiter_attack_ms = 0.1..1000
Set attack time. Default is 1 ms.
.TP
.B limiter_release_ms = 1..9000
Set release time. Default is 50 ms.
.SS Three-band controls
The following controls apply only when limiter_bands is 3.
.TP
.B limiter_low_crossover_hz = 100..2000
Divide the low and mid limiter bands. Default is 500 Hz.
.TP
.B limiter_high_crossover_hz = limiter_low_crossover_hz..5000
Divide the mid and high limiter bands. Default is 2000 Hz.
This must be strictly above the low crossover and below half the active source
sample rate. An invalid active crossover prevents graph activation.
.TP
.B limiter_low_threshold_dbfs = -40..-1
Set low-band limiting threshold. Default is -1.5 dBFS.
.TP
.B limiter_low_ratio = 1..20
Set low-band ratio. Default is 10:1.
.TP
.B limiter_low_knee_db = 0..18
Set low-band soft-knee width. Default is 6 dB.
.TP
.B limiter_low_attack_ms = 0.1..1000
Set low-band attack. Default is 50 ms.
.TP
.B limiter_low_release_ms = 1..9000
Set low-band release. Default is 250 ms.
.TP
.B limiter_mid_threshold_dbfs = -40..-1
Set mid-band limiting threshold. Default is -1.5 dBFS.
.TP
.B limiter_mid_ratio = 1..20
Set mid-band ratio. Default is 10:1.
.TP
.B limiter_mid_knee_db = 0..18
Set mid-band soft-knee width. Default is 6 dB.
.TP
.B limiter_mid_attack_ms = 0.1..1000
Set mid-band attack. Default is 10 ms.
.TP
.B limiter_mid_release_ms = 1..9000
Set mid-band release. Default is 100 ms.
.TP
.B limiter_high_threshold_dbfs = -30..-1
Set high-band limiting threshold. Default is -1.5 dBFS.
.TP
.B limiter_high_ratio = 1..20
Set high-band ratio. Default is 20:1.
.TP
.B limiter_high_knee_db = 0..18
Set high-band soft-knee width. Default is 6 dB.
.TP
.B limiter_high_attack_ms = 0.1..100
Set high-band attack. Default is 0.5 ms.
.TP
.B limiter_high_release_ms = 1..1000
Set high-band release. Default is 25 ms.
.SH FIXED TRANSMITTER PROCESSING
These options apply only to [voice_telemetry]. They do not belong in
stage_order and run in a fixed transmitter-tail order.
.TP
.B lookahead_limiter_enabled = yes|no
Enable or explicitly disable the final limiter. When omitted, the channel
transmit limiter settings determine whether it runs and its ceiling.
.TP
.B lookahead_limiter_ceiling_dbfs = -30..-0.1
Set the final-limiter ceiling. Default is -3 dBFS.
.TP
.B lookahead_limiter_lookahead_ms = 0.1..20
Set final-limiter lookahead. Default is 5 ms.
.TP
.B lookahead_limiter_attack_ms = 0.1..20
Set limiter attack. Default is 1 ms.
.TP
.B lookahead_limiter_release_ms = 1..5000
Set limiter release. Default is 100 ms.
.TP
.B post_limiter_lowpass_enabled = yes|no
Enable the fixed cleanup low-pass after final limiting. Default is no.
.TP
.B post_limiter_lowpass_hz = 5000..20000
Set cleanup-filter cutoff. Default is 8000 Hz.
.SH ORDER OF PROCESSING
Local receiver audio is de-emphasized and squelch-gated, passed through the
receive band-pass and one selected PL filter, optionally passed through RNNoise,
and then passed through the selected dynamics order. Link audio uses only its dynamics graph;
source-specific brick-wall filtering is not available. RNNoise and PL removal
are not available on link or transmitter sources.
.PP
Link processing retains the attached channel's negotiated sample rate and
synchronously processes each eligible current audio frame in place with a
prepared FFmpeg graph and preallocated callback workspace. It uses no
deferred link-rendering thread or input/output handoff queues. Graph
preparation and reload occur outside the callback. A format or capacity
mismatch, unavailable graph, or graph-processing error leaves the current
frame unchanged rather than rebuilding the graph or replaying older audio.
.B radioplus processing stats
reports FFmpeg startup fill, runtime shortfall, synchronous graph errors, and
level measurements. Link processing never asserts or releases PTT.
.PP
The transmitter combines voice and telemetry, applies the voice/telemetry
dynamics graph, and then applies its fixed filtering and peak-control tail.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
[usb]
channel_enabled = yes

[local]
enabled = yes
rnnoise_enabled = yes
stage_order = equalizer,expander,agc,deesser,compressor,limiter
expander_enabled = yes
agc_enabled = yes
ctcss_filter_mode = notch

[link]
enabled = yes
stage_order = equalizer,agc,deesser,compressor
agc_enabled = yes
compressor_enabled = yes

[voice_telemetry]
enabled = yes
input_gain_db = 6.0
stage_order = equalizer,deesser,compressor,limiter
compressor_enabled = yes
limiter_enabled = yes
post_limiter_lowpass_enabled = yes
post_limiter_lowpass_hz = 5000
.fi
.SH FILES
.B /etc/asterisk/usbradioplus.conf
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR usbradioplus (7),
.BR usbradioplus.conf (5),
.BR usbradioplus-tune (8)
